<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Veni Creator Spiritus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century (text); tune first known around <a href="/years/1000">1000</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Veni Creator Spiritus (&quot;Come, Creator Spirit&quot;) is a Christian hymn in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> believed to have been written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hrabanus-maurus">Rabanus Maurus</a>, a ninth-century Frankish Benedictine monk, teacher, archbishop and saint. When the Latin text is used, it is normally sung to a Gregorian chant tune first known from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kempten">Kempten</a> Abbey around the year 1000.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Veni Creator Spiritus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> for Come, Creator Spirit, is an invocation of the Holy Ghost. Several variants of the text exist: the original ninth-century version and a classicised revision published in 1632 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-viii">Pope Urban VIII</a>. The original is used in some Protestant churches and certain Catholic religious orders, and the revision in most secular Catholic parishes. The hymn calls on the Comforter, the gift of God Most High, the fount of life, fire, love and spiritual anointing, and asks for light for the senses, love for the hearts and strength for the weakness of the body. It ends with a doxology to the Father, the Son and the Spirit.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In the Catholic Church the hymn is sung at Terce and Vespers on the feast of Pentecost. It also marks solemn occasions: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/college-of-cardinals">College of Cardinals</a> entering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel">Sistine Chapel</a> for a papal conclave, the consecration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, the ordination of priests, confirmation, the dedication of churches, the convening of synods or councils, the coronation of monarchs and the Red Mass that opens the judicial year. It is traditional to chant it on New Year's Day to obtain a plenary indulgence.</p><p>It has been used in Anglican liturgies since the ordinal of 1550. John Cosin's translation, Come Holy Ghost, our souls inspire, was written for the coronation of Charles I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> in 1625 and has been sung at every British coronation since. At the coronation of Charles III and Camilla in 2023 it was sung for the first time also in Irish Gaelic, Scots Gaelic and Welsh. Thomas Cranmer's version appeared in the 1550 ordinal, and John Dryden's Creator Spirit, by whose aid was published in 1693.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> based his Pentecost chorale Komm, Gott Schöpfer, Heiliger Geist on the hymn, published in 1524 with a melody derived from the chant. Heinrich Bone published a German paraphrase in 1845. There have been more than fifty English translations and paraphrases since the sixteenth century.</p><p>Composers have set the hymn in many forms. They include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marc-antoine-charpentier">Marc-Antoine Charpentier</a>, with five settings, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a>, who harmonised Luther's chorale and built organ preludes on its tune, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hector-berlioz">Hector Berlioz</a>, César Franck, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-bruckner">Anton Bruckner</a>, Maurice Duruflé and Karol Szymanowski. Gustav Mahler set the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> text in Part I of his Symphony No. 8 in E-flat major, completed in 1906, and Paul Hindemith ended his Concerto for Organ and Orchestra with a Phantasy on it in 1962.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Veni Creator Spiritus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
